{"availability":"available","full":"rubys/roundhouse","name":"roundhouse","description":"Rails as a specification; the deployment target is a build flag","category":"Other","language":"Rust","stars":379,"forks":24,"created":"2026-04-17T14:59:50Z","updated":"2026-10-04T08:11:27Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://rubys.github.io/roundhouse/","topics":["compiler","rails","ruby","rust","transpiler","type-inference"],"demo":"https://rubys.github.io/roundhouse/demo/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:37.148Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:37.148Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:37.148Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-04T08:15:37.148Z","stars":379,"forks":24}],"technologies":["Rust"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Rails as a specification; deployment is a build flag.","Roundhouse reads Ruby source — specifically, Rails applications — and produces standalone projects in other target languages. The deployment target (Rust or Swift binary, TypeScript bundle, Crystal or Go service, Elixir OTP app, Kotlin/JVM or C#/.NET service, Python project, browser bundle, or Spinel-compiled Ruby) becomes a compiler flag rather than a runtime choice.","A roundhouse is the circular hub in a rail yard where engines rotate and route onto different tracks. That's the pipeline shape: one Ruby source at the center, analyzed and dispatched to one of N target tracks."],"features":["Conformance vs Comprehension — the project, and the conformance-oracle methodology behind it","Live Types for Rails — the inference as a live type checker: LSP, MCP, and the competitive landscape","An IDE You Don't Install — Mastodon analyzed in a browser tab","Campfire Chats — a real product, compiled","The Ruby JRuby Was Built to Run — the 2×2 experiment the table above is the current round of"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/rubys/roundhouse/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"9bf8d5be5cddcd373ef6608341b77b3773c68007efdf2d72606e48